Sustainable behavior design in refurbishment contexts focuses on how products and systems can encourage users to engage in repair, maintenance, and responsible consumption practices throughout product lifecycles. Rather than relying solely on information or moral appeals, behavioral design embeds cues, feedback, and incentive structures directly into product experiences and service interactions. This approach recognizes that sustainability outcomes are driven not just by individual intentions but by the systemic affordances that make sustainable choices convenient, visible, and rewarding.

In refurbishment contexts, sustainable behavior design influences decisions around participation in repair services, engagement with refurbished products, and proper maintenance practices. Visible care instructions, satisfaction feedback from repaired products, loyalty incentives for repair engagement, and transparent communication about refurbishment processes all shape user participation. The framework connects to broader principles of service design and ecosystem thinking, where multiple touchpoints work together to establish circular practices as normalized behaviors.